-- title:
--   Level automorphisms stabilise the blow-up centre and chart
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$ (`hqM'`), and $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell\equiv 11\pmod{12}$ (`hℓ12`) and $\ell\mid M'$ (`hℓM'`).
--
--   **Level data.** $L$ is a field of characteristic $0$ and $\zeta\in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hζ`); `hι` asserts the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota(\zeta)=\exp(2\pi i/q)$. The subgroup $H_1\le(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ is required by `hH₁` to be the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/\ell)^\times$ (available since $\ell\mid M'\mid q^2M'$). The intermediate field $K$ of $L\subset\operatorname{LaurentSeries}L$ is required by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q^2*M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of $\operatorname{LaurentSeries}L$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ over $\mathbb Q$.
--
--   **Base ring.** $A$ is a discrete valuation domain which is a Henselian local ring with algebraically closed residue field, $L$ is its fraction field, $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAq`), and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$ (`hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with the tower $A\to L\to K$. The element $\varpi\in A$ generates the maximal ideal (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t\in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\,u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   **The chart and the supersingular point.** $j\in K$ is a nonzero element whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the image over $L$ of the $q$-expansion $t^{-1}\cdot\mathtt{jNumQ}$ of the modular invariant (`hj`). Write $C:=$ `chartAlgFin A (↥K) j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and $\mathfrak X:=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A (↥K) j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout of $\operatorname{Spec}C$ and $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the corresponding algebra for $j^{-1}$ along the middle chart. The ideal $y\subset C$ is maximal (`hy`) and contains the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and `hss` requires: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of those $j_0\in\Omega$ for which every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j_0$ has no nonzero affine point killed by $q$.
--
--   The point $z$ of $\mathfrak X$ carries the germ $\varpi_z$ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under the structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec}A$ (`hϖz`), and $\varpi_z$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk of $\mathfrak X$ at $z$ (`hz`). The point $y'$ of `XFin A (↥K) j` $=\operatorname{Spec}C$ maps to $z$ under the canonical morphism `ιFin` (`hy'`), satisfies the same supersingularity condition at its ideal (`hss'`), and `hy'y` identifies $y'{}.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ with $y$.
--
--   **The Drinfeld chart witness.** $W_1$ is a discrete valuation domain, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, $\sigma_1:A\to W_1$ a ring homomorphism with $\mathfrak m_{W_1}=(\sigma_1\varpi)$ (`hσ₁`); $f_1,u_1,v_1\in W_1[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u_1,v_1$ units and $f_1$ congruent to [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W₁`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18) $=X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf₁`); and $e_1$ is a ring isomorphism from the adic completion of the stalk of $\mathfrak X$ at $z$ with respect to its maximal ideal onto
--   $$S:=W_1[[X_0,X_1]]\big/\big(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v_1-f_1u_1\big).$$
--   Write $\mathrm{toC}$ for the map from the stalk to its completion, $\mathrm{mk}_S$ for the quotient map onto $S$, $\mathrm{germY}:C\to$ stalk for the canonical map (the inverse of the global-sections isomorphism of $\operatorname{Spec}C$, followed by the inverse of the `appIso` of `ιFin` on the top open and the germ at $z$), and $\Psi:=e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}:C\to S$; put $\mathfrak b:=(\mathrm{mk}_S\,C(\sigma_1\varpi_t),\ \mathrm{mk}_S X_0,\ \mathrm{mk}_S X_1)\subset S$. Throughout, for $\gamma\in SL_2(\mathbb Z)$ and $\tau$ an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$, the predicate [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) says: for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$, $p_g$ having nonzero associated Laurent series, every $x\in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the image over $L$ of the ratio of those series, and every ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota(\zeta)=\exp(2\pi i/q)$, one has $\iota(\tau x)\cdot\mathrm{qExp}\big(g\mid_k\widetilde{\gamma^{-1}}\big)=\mathrm{qExp}\big(f\mid_k\widetilde{\gamma^{-1}}\big)$, where $\widetilde{\delta}=$ `conjElemN q δ` is the matrix $\begin{pmatrix}\delta_{00}&\delta_{01}/q\\ q\,\delta_{10}&\delta_{11}\end{pmatrix}$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hW₁` is a conjunction of seven clauses about this witness, of which the contents are as follows. (i) *Constants*: for every $a\in A$, $e_1$ carries the image in the completion of the germ at $z$ of $a$ (via `toBase`) to $\mathrm{mk}_S\,C(\sigma_1 a)$. (ii) *Chart stability*: for every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau$ with `IsLevelAutAt … γ⁻¹ K τ`, the subalgebra $C$ is stable under $\tau$. (iii) *Fixing the point*: for every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ with $\gamma_{11}\equiv1\pmod\ell$, every such $\tau$ preserving $C$, and every $a\in C$, the difference $\tau a-a$ lies in $y'{}.\mathrm{asIdeal}$. (iv) *Linearisation*: for every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every such $\tau$ preserving $C$ and acting trivially modulo $y'{}.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c\in W_1$ and a matrix $M\in M_2(W_1)$ such that $\theta\circ\Psi=\Psi\circ\tau|_C$; $\theta$ fixes every constant $\mathrm{mk}_S\,C(w)$; for each coordinate $j_0$, $\theta(\mathrm{mk}_SX_{j_0})-\mathrm{mk}_S\big(\sum_i C(M_{i j_0})X_i\big)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mk}_SX_0,\mathrm{mk}_SX_1$; $c^{q+1}\equiv1$, and $M_{ij}\equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$, modulo $\mathfrak m_{W_1}$; if $\gamma_{11}\equiv1\pmod\ell$ then $c\equiv1$ modulo $\mathfrak m_{W_1}$; and if $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ while $\tau$ is not the identity then $c-1\notin\mathfrak m_{W_1}$. (v) *Separation of tangent directions*: for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each failing to contain both $\mathrm{mk}_SX_0$ and $\mathrm{mk}_SX_1$, each containing $\mathrm{mk}_S\,C(\sigma_1\varpi)$, and each containing an element $\mathrm{mk}_S\big(C(a_i)X_0+C(b_i)X_1+h\big)$ with $h\in(X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ along $e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}$ are distinct. (vi) *Coefficient criterion*: for a prime $P$ of $S$ not containing both $\mathrm{mk}_SX_0,\mathrm{mk}_SX_1$, containing $\mathrm{mk}_S\,C(\sigma_1\varpi)$ and containing some $\mathrm{mk}_S\big(C(1)X_0+C(0)X_1+h\big)$ with $h\in(X_0,X_1)^2$, and for $a\in C$: $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a)$ lies in the contraction of $P$ along $e_1$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$. (vii) *Witness at the Hasse invariant*: [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) $=$ `qExpand L q (jqModC L)` lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0\in A$ with that element congruent to $a_0$ modulo $y'{}.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, an integer $e_0\ge1$ and $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that for all $a,b\in W_1$ not both in $\mathfrak m_{W_1}$ with $a^qb-ab^q\in\mathfrak m_{W_1}$ the form $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0}\mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit, and $\Psi$ of that element minus $a_0$ equals $\mathrm{mk}_S h$.
--
--   **The centre and the blown-up chart.** The ideal $J\subset C$ is required by `hJ` to be the infimum of the set of ideals of the form $(\tau|_C)^{-1}\big(\Psi^{-1}(\mathfrak b)\big)$, taken over all $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and all $\tau$ with `IsLevelAutAt … γ⁻¹ K τ` preserving $C$. The $A$-subalgebra $B\subset K$ is required by `hB` to be the $A$-restriction of scalars of the $C$-subalgebra of $K$ generated by $\{x\in K:\ x\,\varpi_t\in J\}$, that is $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Three assertions hold.
--
--   1.
--
--   For every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ` which preserves $C$ and satisfies $\tau a-a\in y$ for all $a\in C$: for every $a\in C$, $\Psi(a)\in\mathfrak b$ if and only if $\Psi(\tau a)\in\mathfrak b$.
--
--   2.
--
--   For every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, every $\tau$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`, and every $a\in C$ with $\tau a\in C$: $a\in J$ if and only if $\tau a\in J$.
--
--   3.
--
--   For every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, every $\tau$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`, and every $f\in B$: $\tau f\in B$.
--
--   Thus the local centre $\Psi^{-1}(\mathfrak b)$ is stable under the level automorphisms fixing the supersingular point modulo $y$, and the globalised centre $J$ together with the blown-up chart algebra $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$ are stable under all level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$.
--
--   This is the equivariance step in the construction of an integral model of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ over the Henselian base $A$ at a supersingular point, where the Drinfeld chart is modified by a weighted blow-up with centre $J$: it states that the centre and the blown-up chart algebra are preserved by the automorphisms of the function field induced by $\Gamma_0(M')$, in the auxiliary $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-frame with a guard prime $\ell\equiv11\pmod{12}$, valid for all primes $q$. It is used by the downstream analyses of the decomposition and inertia action on the components of the Drinfeld fibre and of the primes above a supersingular point.
